        "body":  "Thanks for running this on real 3FS with two hosts - that covers the two things I could not test here (two writers, one metadata server, and a pool that actually fills). The numbers answer what a single-host harness cannot: each flood prompt evicting exactly its 50 keys, 0/29 unleased vs 29/29 leased surviving, and the expired lease reclaimed on the next flood.\n\nOn (1): you are right, and it is now written down. Leases are counted per key, and a key stays pinned until the latest deadline among its live leases, so one reader releasing does not unpin it for another reader. Your 125 s observation is the same rule seen from the other side: pod B\u0027s read at ~10 s refreshed the deadline, so the pin lasted until last-touch + TTL, not acquire + TTL. I added that sentence to `acquire_keys` and a test (`test_overlapping_leases_keep_the_key_pinned`: acquire with 10 s, then 1e6 s, release the first, require that allocation still fails; release the second, require that it succeeds). Pushed as c9b2b40 - docstring and test only, no behaviour change, so the sha moves from ed8a5db to c9b2b40. Local isolated harness: 26 passed, ruff clean. Any test you re-run on c9b2b40 is testing the same code paths.\n\nOn (2) I do not have a proven explanation either. The 100/50 free-page split plus \"newest three not found\" looks like allocation that partly succeeded and was then released: `allocate_pages_for_keys` is all-or-nothing per rank, so during a 5x flood some keys can fail to allocate (or their write can fail) and their pages return to the free list, leaving the newest prompts never complete - which a reader sees exactly as \"not found\". The alternative is that they completed and were then evicted; LRU makes that unlikely for the newest keys, but not impossible if allocation continued after them.\n\nTwo things from that window would separate the cases:\n- any `Evicted key ...` lines between the flood and the check (eviction) versus none (never completed);\n- whether the save-failure counter on the writing pod moved, and whether the miss was at the scheduler (`batch_key_exists` False: absent or partial) or in the worker (complete but pages gone).\n\nIf it is reproducible, I would rather add one debug-level log line, \"eviction had no candidate\" / \"allocation failed after releasing pages\", than grow the PR. Should I add that, or would you prefer the next run to start from c9b2b40 as is?\n",
